+## Call another app
+
+An app can call tools of another installed app it declares as a dependency.
+Declare it in the requirements, next to `app` and `user` accounts. Keep
+`as const` so the literal `scope` and `kind` values type-check:
+
+```ts
+import { defineApp, object, query, router, string } from "apps";
+
+const requirements = {
+  dependencies: {
+    tasks: {
+      scope: "user", // "app": one connection for everyone; "user": each member saves their own
+      tools: {
+        search: { kind: "query" },
+        create: { kind: "mutation" },
+      },
+    },
+  },
+} as const;
+
+const find = query(
+  { input: object({ target: string(), text: string() }) },
+  async (ctx, { target, text }) =>
+    ctx.apps.call({ connection: "tasks", target, tool: "search", kind: "query", input: { text } }),
+);
+
+export default defineApp(requirements, { tools: router({ find }) });
+```
+
+A dependency name has 1 to 48 letters, digits, `_` or `-` and starts with a
+letter. `tools` lists every target tool the app may call with its `kind`;
+optional `inputSchema` and `outputSchema` (JSON Schema) are checked on every
+call. `cardinality: "many"` lets one dependency hold a connection per target
+app; the default `"one"` holds a single target.
+
+A declaration grants nothing until someone saves a connection that binds it to
+a target app. On hosted Executor use the management app:
+
+```js
+return await tools.executor.appConnections.save({
+  path: {
+    organization: "<approved-organization-id>",
+    app: "<calling-app-id>",
+    dependency: "tasks",
+  },
+  body: { scope: "user", target: "<target-app-id>", revision: 0 },
+});
+```
+
+`revision` is the connection's current revision: `0` creates it, and each save
+returns the next one. A stale value fails with `AppConnectionConflict`.
+`appConnections.list`, `update` (with `enabled`) and `remove` (with `scope` and
+`revision` in `query`) manage saved connections; with `cardinality: "many"`,
+also pass `target`. An `app` connection needs edit access to the calling app; a
+`user` connection needs only use of it. Either needs use of the target app.
+
+The call runs as the person who saved the connection, with the accounts the
+connection names in `accounts`, keyed by the scope and name of the target's
+account requirements: `{ app: { gmail: ["<account-id>"] }, user: { notion:
+["<account-id>"] } }`. `app` is required, even as `{}`. With a `user` key, even
+`{}`, the target runs as that
+person and every one of its requirements must be named. Leave `user` out to run
+the target as the app, which then needs only its `app` requirements. A target
+with no account requirements needs no `accounts`. Saving does not check this;
+a missing account fails the call with `denied`. Read the target's requirements
+with `apps.get` first.
+
+`ctx.apps.call({ connection, target, tool, kind, input })` returns the target
+tool's result. `target` is the target app's ID and must match the saved
+connection; pass it in the tool's or workflow's input, or store it in app data.
+A call fails with `AppCallFailed`, whose `reason` says why, and is never resent
+automatically:
+
+- `denied`: the tool or kind is not declared, the connection is disabled or
+  names another target, a `user` dependency was called without a caller, an
+  account is missing, the target asked for approval or is disabled, or the
+  target tool failed.
+- `connection_missing`: no connection is saved for this dependency, scope and caller.
+- `stale`: the calling app was redeployed during the call, or the dependency's
+  declaration changed since the connection was saved. Save it again.
+- `invalid_input` or `invalid_output`: the values do not match the declared schemas.
+- `effects_unavailable`: a mutation was called where writes are not allowed, or
+  the target's build is too old to accept one; see below.
+- `in_progress`, `identity_conflict` or `outcome_unknown`: a write with the same
+  step identity is running, was sent with other input, or may have happened.
+  Check the target before doing anything else.
+- `device_offline`: the target needs an account reached through a device that
+  is offline.
+- `depth_exceeded`: calls nested more than eight deep.
+
+A query may call only `query` tools. A `mutation` tool can be called only from
+a workflow step: `ctx.step.do("name", (step) => step.apps.call({ ... }))`, or a
+mutation that `ctx.step.runMutation` runs. The step's identity becomes the
+write's receipt, so a replayed step returns the first result instead of writing
+again. A mutation called from a tool, schedule or webhook handler fails with
+`effects_unavailable` before anything is sent.
+
+A dependency with `scope: "user"` needs a caller, so app schedules and other
+app-scope work cannot use it. Nobody can answer an approval inside another
+app's call: a target tool whose approval returns `user-approval` is refused
+with `denied`, not paused. A `never-ask` tool policy for that app, tool or
+account, optionally limited to one workflow, approves it instead. Most catalog
+apps ask before every mutation, so a cross-app write usually needs such a
+policy first. People create tool policies in the signed-in dashboard; the
+management app can only list them.
+
+## Request a model
+
+Hosted Executor can lend an app a model through a model pool. Declare a model
+requirement, then call it from a mutation:
+
+```ts
+import { defineApp, mutation, object, router, string, type MutationContext } from "apps";
+
+const requirements = {
+  models: {
+    writer: { scope: "user", limits: { maxOutputTokens: 400 } },
+  },
+} as const;
+
+const summarize = mutation(
+  { input: object({ text: string() }) },
+  async (ctx: MutationContext<typeof requirements>, { text }) => {
+    const reply = await ctx.models.writer.request({
+      messages: [
+        { role: "system", content: "Summarize in one sentence." },
+        { role: "user", content: text },
+      ],
+    });
+    return { summary: reply.choices[0].message.content };
+  },
+);
+
+export default defineApp(requirements, { tools: router({ summarize }) });
+```
+
+A request is one non-streaming Chat Completions exchange: `messages` (roles
+`system`, `developer`, `user`, `assistant`), and optional `model`, `max_tokens`,
+`temperature` and `response_format` (`text`, `json_object` or `json_schema`).
+The reply has one `choices` entry and `usage` when the provider reports it.
+Annotate the handler's context with `MutationContext<typeof requirements>`, or
+`AnyScopeMutationContext<typeof requirements>` for one schedules also run, so
+`ctx.models` has the declared names.
+
+A request needs a model name. The first of the request's `model`, the binding's
+`model` and the requirement's `model` is used; with none, the request fails
+with `invalid_request`. Each limit (`timeoutMs`, `maxInputBytes`,
+`maxOutputTokens`) is the lower of the requirement's and the binding's. The
+binding's default to 30 seconds, 32,768 bytes and 800 tokens, so only a binding
+can raise them. Without `max_tokens` a request asks for the output limit; a
+larger `max_tokens` fails with `invalid_request`.
+
+A requirement does nothing until it is bound to a pool. `models.list` returns
+the app's declarations, bindings and the pools you can use. Bind one, naming a
+model the pool serves:
+
+```js
+return await tools.executor.models.bind({
+  path: { organization: "<approved-organization-id>", app: "<app-id>", requirement: "writer" },
+  body: { scope: "user", pool: "<pool-app-id>", model: "<model-name>" },
+});
+```
+
+`scope` must match the declaration. An `app` binding needs edit access to the
+app and serves everyone; each member binds a `user` requirement for themselves.
+`limits` in the body sets the binding's limits. `models.unbind`, with `scope`
+in `query`, removes a binding. Usage is charged to the bound pool.
+
+A `user` requirement needs a caller: app schedules and app-account webhooks get
+`denied`, so give background work an `app` requirement. Queries have no
+`ctx.models`. Every failure is `ModelRequestFailed` with a `reason`:
+`durable_steps_unavailable` inside a workflow, because replaying a step could
+send and charge a request twice (start a mutation outside the workflow
+instead), or `binding_missing`, `denied`, `pool_unavailable`, `cap_exceeded`,
+`invalid_request`, `invalid_response`, `cancelled`, `timeout` or
+`outcome_unknown`. None is retried automatically; after `timeout` or
+`outcome_unknown` the request may have been charged.
+
+## Use the app's drive
+
+On Executor Cloud an app that declares `drive: true` in its requirements gets
+its own versioned file store as `ctx.drive`. Each change is a commit, identified
+by a 40-character `oid`. Other hosts answer every drive call with
+`DriveRequestFailed` and `reason: "unavailable"`.
+
+Queries read with `list`, `read`, `search`, `history`, `attachment` and
+`devices`. Mutations and webhooks also write with `write`, `apply`, `move`,
+`remove`, `restore` and `resolve`. A write from a query fails with `denied`.
+
+```ts
+const page = await ctx.drive.list({ path: "notes" });
+const note = await ctx.drive.read({ path: "notes/today.md" });
+const result = await ctx.drive.write({
+  base: page.at,
+  path: "notes/today.md",
+  content: "Ship it.",
+  mode: note === null ? "create" : "edit",
+  operation: "note-today-1",
+});
+```
+
+Every write names the `base` commit it edits and an `operation` key of at most
+128 characters. Retrying the same change with the same key returns the first
+result instead of writing again; use a new key for each new change.
+`receipt({ operation })` reads a write's outcome later. A write returns
+`landed` with the new `oid`; changes since `base` that touch other content
+merge. It returns `conflict` only for paths that clash, each with a `reason` of
+`content` or `unsupported`. Read again and retry, or settle them with
+`resolve`. `apply` changes up
+to 100 files at once. `devices()` lists each device grant's last sync and the
+head it saw then; a device is up to date when that head is the drive's `head`.
